# Named `Dockerfile.dockerignore` (BuildKit's Dockerfile-specific ignore
# file, colocated with the Dockerfile it governs) rather than a plain
# `.dockerignore` in this same directory: empirically verified (see the
# task report) that `docker build -f deploy/custody-host/Dockerfile -t
# elastos-custody-host .` (context root `.`, per the build command this
# image is built with) does NOT honor a `.dockerignore` placed in the
# Dockerfile's own subdirectory -- only a context-root `.dockerignore` or
# this `<dockerfile-name>.dockerignore` form is read. A root-level
# `.dockerignore` is out of scope (only new files under deploy/custody-host/
# for this task), and without a working ignore file here `docker build`
# would try to send target-build/'s tens of GB as build context.
target
target-capsules
target-build
*/target
capsules/*/target
.git
